course: dictionary
lesson: 5305
title: A word from Japan
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Karate is a word from Japan. It names a martial art and sport where people fight using hands, feet, elbows and knees, and it also names the skills and training that go with it. Context helps you choose the right meaning.
explain: «Martial arts start with control and respect: judo's first lesson is how to fall safely, and karate teaches you to avoid fights and use your skills only to get away from danger.» The clue words are control, respect, safely and avoid. They show that karate is about self-control and staying safe, not about hurting people.
explain: Karate is an uncountable noun, so we say «do karate» or «a karate class», not «a karate». Belts show the level a student has reached, from white up to black, so «a black belt in karate» means a very high level.
fact: Karate is a Japanese martial art and sport.
fact: In karate, people fight using hands, feet, elbows and knees, with strikes, kicks and blocks.
fact: Karate also means the skills and training of the sport.
fact: Belts show levels, from white belt up to black belt.
fact: Karate is practised with rules, a teacher and control. You should never use moves to hurt people for fun.
exercise: «Many children do karate after school.» What is karate in this sentence? | A school subject like maths | *A Japanese martial art and sport | A kind of bread from Japan | A card game played in a circle
exercise: Belts in karate go from white up to which colour? | Yellow | Green | *Black | White
exercise: «Karate teaches you to avoid fights.» What does karate teach you to do? | Fight for fun | Start fights to win | *Avoid fights and stay safe | Show off to your friends
test: Which phrase is correct? | karate's | *do karate | a karate | karates
test: Why should karate moves only be used in self-defence? | To win every argument | *To get away from danger and keep people safe | To show off in class | To hurt people for fun
exam: In stories, a karate expert can be what? | *A hero or a comic show-off | A chef who only cooks soup | A wizard who turns into a frog | A bus driver who never stops
